= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $\mathcal O=\{x:|x|\leq1\}$. First, $\mathcal O$ is compact. A compact neighborhood of zero contains a sufficiently small closed ball $t^m\mathcal O$, since $|t|^m\to0$; that ball is closed in the compact neighborhood. Scaling back proves compactness of $\mathcal O$. Also $K$ is complete: a <Cauchy sequence> has a tail in a translate of a compact neighborhood, hence a convergent subsequence, and the Cauchy condition makes the whole sequence converge. This is the <completeness of locally compact nontrivially valued fields> argument.

The additive subgroup $t\mathcal O$ is open: a sufficiently small ball around any of its points stays in $t\mathcal O$ by the ultrametric inequality. Thus the compact discrete quotient $\mathcal O/t\mathcal O$ is finite. Choose a set $A\subset\mathcal O$ of representatives, with zero representing the zero coset. For $x\in\mathcal O$, recursively choose $a_n\in A$ and $x_{n+1}\in\mathcal O$ such that $x_n=a_n+t x_{n+1}$, beginning with $x_0=x$. Then
$$
x=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}a_jt^j+t^m x_m,\qquad |t^m x_m|\leq|t|^m\longrightarrow0.
$$
For arbitrary $x\in K$, multiply first by a large power of $t$ to put it in $\mathcal O$, then divide the resulting expansion by that power. This gives the <digit expansion with a nonuniformizer>.

For uniqueness, take the first exponent $r$ at which two expansions differ. After dividing their difference by $t^r$, all the later terms belong to the closed subgroup $t\mathcal O$, whereas $a_r-b_r$ does not, because the representatives are distinct. The difference cannot be zero. Conversely every series with coefficients in $A$ and only finitely many nonzero negative-index terms converges by completeness and $|t|<1$. **Every element therefore has a unique expansion $\boxed{x=\sum_{n\geq r}a_n t^n}$**, with the harmless initial zero coefficients understood as part of the same indexed series. No assumption that $t$ is a <uniformizer> is needed.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The compactness and completeness proved above apply again. The finite ring $\mathcal O/t\mathcal O$ has a positive integer characteristic $m$, so $m\in t\mathcal O$ and $|m|<1$. Prime factorization supplies a prime $p$ with $|p|<1$. In characteristic zero the restriction to $\mathbb Q$ is therefore a positive power of the <p-adic absolute value>, by the preceding classification. Completing $\mathbb Q$ embeds the <p-adic number> field $\mathbb Q_p$ into the complete field $K$.

Now $p\mathcal O$ is open, so $\mathcal O/p\mathcal O$ is finite. It is an $\mathbb F_p$-vector space; choose lifts $e_1,\ldots,e_d\in\mathcal O$ of a basis. Successively reducing modulo $p\mathcal O$ gives, for every $x\in\mathcal O$,
$$
x=\sum_{n\geq0}p^n\sum_{j=1}^d a_{j,n}e_j=\sum_{j=1}^d c_j e_j,\qquad a_{j,n}\in\{0,\ldots,p-1\},\quad c_j=\sum_{n\geq0}a_{j,n}p^n\in\mathbb Z_p.
$$
Convergence in $K$ follows from $|p|<1$. Every element of $K$ becomes integral after multiplication by a power of $p$, so $K=\sum_j\mathbb Q_p e_j$. **Consequently $\boxed{[K:\mathbb Q_p]<\infty}$: $K$ is a <p-adic field>.** This finite-module proof does not assume discreteness of the original value group in advance.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take the <formal Laurent series field> $K=\mathbb C((T))$, with $v_T$ the least exponent of a nonzero term and $|x|=2^{-v_T(x)}$. Its value group is $\mathbb Z$, so this is a <discrete valuation>. A <Cauchy sequence> has an eventually stable coefficient at every exponent, and its tail has a common lower bound on its exponents. The stabilized coefficients therefore give a <formal Laurent series field> element to which the sequence converges. Hence $K$ is complete.

Its <valuation ring> is $\mathbb C[[T]]$, and its <residue field> is the infinite field $\mathbb C$. If $K$ were locally compact, the previous compact-ball argument would make $\mathbb C[[T]]$ compact. Its quotient by the open ideal $T\mathbb C[[T]]$ would then be compact and discrete, hence finite, contradicting its identification with $\mathbb C$. **Thus $\boxed{\mathbb C((T))\text{ is complete and discretely valued but not locally compact}}$.**

= 2
{parent=Paper 28}
