= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A useful version is the <strong form of Hensel lemma>. Let $K$ be complete for a <non-Archimedean absolute value>, let $f\in\mathcal O_K[X]$, and suppose $a\in\mathcal O_K$ satisfies
$$
|f(a)|<|f'(a)|^2.
$$
Then there is a unique root $\alpha$ in the ball $|\alpha-a|<|f'(a)|$, and $|\alpha-a|\leq|f(a)/f'(a)|$.

Put $h=|f'(a)|$ and $\eta=|f(a)|/h^2<1$. Starting with $x_0=a$, apply <Newton iteration over a valued field> $x_{n+1}=x_n-f(x_n)/f'(x_n)$. Taylor expansion over the <valuation ring> gives
$$
|f(x+u)-f(x)-f'(x)u|\leq|u|^2,\qquad |f'(x+u)-f'(x)|\leq|u|
$$
for integral $x,u$. Inductively $|f'(x_n)|=h$ and $|f(x_n)|/h^2\leq\eta^{2^n}$. Indeed the Newton increment has size at most $h\eta^{2^n}<h$, so the derivative size is unchanged, and the new function value is bounded by the square of the increment. The increments tend to zero, and the ultrametric inequality makes the sequence Cauchy. Completeness supplies a root with the stated distance bound.

If $b,c$ are distinct points of the ball, their divided difference is $f'(a)$ plus a term of size at most $\max(|b-a|,|c-a|)<h$, so $(f(b)-f(c))/(b-c)$ is nonzero. Two roots in that ball are impossible. **This proves the stated <Hensel lemma>.** In particular a simple root modulo the <maximal ideal> lifts uniquely to a root in its residue class.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $p=2$, the even residue class contains exactly one root, by the simple-root form of <Hensel lemma> at $a=0$: $f(0)=20$ and $f'(0)=-5$ is a unit. For the odd classes use the <strong form of Hensel lemma>:
$$
f(1)=16,\quad f'(1)=-2;\qquad f(3)=32,\quad f'(3)=22.
$$
In both cases $v_2(f(a))>2v_2(f'(a))=2$. Each gives exactly one root in $a+4\mathbb Z_2$. These two balls partition the odd integers, so there are no further roots.

For $p=3$, reduction gives $\overline f=X^3+X+2$, whose sole root is $2$. The derivative $3X^2-5$ is always a unit modulo $3$, so that root lifts uniquely. For $p=5$, the only possible residue is zero. But if $x\in5\mathbb Z_5$, then $x^3-5x\in25\mathbb Z_5$ while $20$ has valuation one, so $f(x)$ cannot vanish. **The exact root counts are**
$$
\boxed{\#\{f=0\text{ in }\mathbb Z_2\}=3,\quad \#\{f=0\text{ in }\mathbb Z_3\}=1,\quad \#\{f=0\text{ in }\mathbb Z_5\}=0.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The polynomial is an <Eisenstein polynomial> at $5$, so it is irreducible and $[K:\mathbb Q]=3$. Its <polynomial discriminant> is
$$
\operatorname{disc}(f)=500-10800=-10300=-4\cdot5^2\cdot103.
$$
One must remove the index at $2$ before using this as the <field discriminant>. Put $\beta=(\alpha^2+\alpha)/2$. Direct reduction using $\alpha^3=5\alpha-20$ gives
$$
\alpha^2=2\beta-\alpha,\quad \alpha\beta=2\alpha+\beta-10,\quad \beta^2=3\beta-4\alpha-10.
$$
Thus $B=\mathbb Z+\mathbb Z\alpha+\mathbb Z\beta$ is a ring finite over $\mathbb Z$, so its elements are integral. Its basis has <discriminant of elements of a number field> $-2575=-5^2\cdot103$. The <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice> says $\operatorname{disc}(B)=[\mathcal O_K:B]^2\Delta_K$, so only $5$ could divide the remaining index.

We use these standard <different ideal> facts: the norm of the different is the absolute <field discriminant>; its localization is the local different; an unramified prime has different exponent zero, and a tamely ramified prime of <ramification index> $e$ has different exponent $e-1$. The <Eisenstein polynomial> at $5$ gives total ramification of degree three, which is tame because $5\nmid3$. Its contribution to $v_5(\Delta_K)$ is therefore two. This excludes a further index factor $5$, proving **$\mathcal O_K=B$ and $\boxed{\Delta_K=-2575}$.**

Since the index $[\mathcal O_K:\mathbb Z[\alpha]]=2$ is prime to $5$ and $103$, <Dedekind factorization theorem> applies at both primes. Modulo $5$, $f=X^3$, giving a single prime $\mathfrak p=(5,\alpha)$ with <residue degree> one and <ramification index> three. Modulo $103$,
$$
f(X)=(X-6)^2(X+12).
$$
Thus $\mathfrak q=(103,\alpha-6)$ has <residue degree> one and <ramification index> two; the other prime over $103$ is unramified. Both ramified primes are tame. No other prime ramifies because none divides $\Delta_K$. **Consequently**
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak D_{K/\mathbb Q}=\mathfrak p^2\mathfrak q,\qquad N\mathfrak p=5,\quad N\mathfrak q=103.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 28}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose $a\in K^\times$ with $0<|a|_1<1$. For any $x\ne0$, the powers $x^n$ tend to zero precisely when $|x|_1<1$. Equality of the two topologies therefore implies
$$
|x|_1<1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad |x|_2<1.
$$
In particular $0<|a|_2<1$. For positive integers $n$ and arbitrary integers $m$, apply this equivalence to $x^n a^{-m}$. It says that
$$
\frac{\log|x|_1}{\log|a|_1}>\frac mn\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \frac{\log|x|_2}{\log|a|_2}>\frac mn.
$$
The inequality reverses on division by either negative logarithm. The rational cuts of the two real numbers are equal, so density of $\mathbb Q$ makes the numbers equal. Hence **the <equivalent absolute values> satisfy**
$$
\boxed{|x|_2=|x|_1^c,\qquad c=\frac{\log|a|_2}{\log|a|_1}>0.}
$$
The formula also holds at zero. Nontriviality is essential to choosing $a$; no Archimedean or non-Archimedean restriction was used in the proof.

